dth": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 13.4, "y": 24.4, "width": 67.2, "height": 23.9, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 13.4, "y": 24.4, "width": 67.2, "height": 23.9, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 13.4, "y": 24.4, "width": 67.2, "height": 23.9, "rotation": 0, "text": ["Definition (Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference (WARP))\nLet (p^1, x^1) and (p^2, x^2) be a pair of observations on prices and consumption bundles, with p^1 \u00bb 0 and x^t \u2265 0 for t = 1, 2 and such that (p^1, x^1) \u2260 (p^2, x^2). We say that individual choices (given by x^i) satisfy the Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference if, whenever p^2 x^1 < p^2 x^2, we must then have that p^1 x^2 > p^1 x^1."]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 13.4, "y": 51.6, "width": 66.4, "height": 37.8, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 13.4, "y": 51.6, "width": 66.4, "height": 37.8, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 13.4, "y": 51.6, "width": 66.4, "height": 37.8, "rotation": 0, "text": ["The idea of this definition is fairly intuitive: take the price vector p^2; at those prices, the consumer chooses the bundle x^2. However, x^1 was also available at those prices (i.e. p^2 x^1 \u2264 p^2 x^2), but was not chosen. This implies that the consumer preferred x^2 over x^1 (so, she revealed her preference). Therefore, it must be the case that when prices were p^1, x^2 could not be available for consumption, because if that were the case, then she would again choose x^2 instead of x^1. Figure 4 illustrates an example in which the WARP is NOT satisfied."]}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 22.388980263157894, "y": 0.9868421052631579, "width": 45.14802631578948, "height": 2.6315789473684212, "rotation": 0}, "id": "hfiaukJXBW", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "manual"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 22.388980263157894, "y": 0.9868421052631579, "width": 45.14802631578948, "height": 2.6315789473684212, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "hfiaukJXBW", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "manual"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 22.388980263157894, "y": 0.9868421052631579, "width": 45.14802631578948, "height": 2.6315789473684212, "rotation": 0, "text": ["Testing Individual Rationality with Finite Data Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference"]}, "id": "hfiaukJXBW", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "manual"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 16.097861842105264, "y": 92.43421052631578, "width": 14.710115131578945, "height": 2.30263157894737, "rotation": 0}, "id": "hpEiVfHlXF", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "manual"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 16.097861842105264, "